      "text": "To find the value of \\(a\\) such that the lines given by the equations \\(y = 2x + 13\\) and \\(6y + ax = 6\\) are perpendicular, we need to determine the slopes of both lines and use the property that the slopes of two perpendicular lines are negative reciprocals of each other.\n\nFirst, let's find the slope of the first line. The equation of the first line is already in slope-intercept form \\(y = 2x + 13\\). Therefore, the slope \\(m_1\\) of the first line is \\(2\\).\n\nNext, we need to rewrite the second equation \\(6y + ax = 6\\) in slope-intercept form \\(y = mx + b\\). We do this by solving for \\(y\\):\n\n\\[\n6y + ax = 6\n\\]\n\\[\n6y = -ax + 6\n\\]\n\\[\ny = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1\n\\]\n\nSo, the slope \\(m_2\\) of the second line is \\(-\\frac{a}{6}\\).\n\nFor the lines to be perpendicular, the product of their slopes must be \\(-1\\):\n\n\\[\nm_1 \\cdot m_2 = -1\n\\]\n\\[\n2 \\cdot \\left(-\\frac{a}{6}\\right) = -1\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\frac{2a}{6} = -1\n\\]\n\\[\n-\\frac{a}{3} = -1\n\\]\n\\[\na = 3\n\\]\n\nThus, the value of \\(a\\) that makes the lines perpendicular is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
