      "text": "To determine the value of \\( a \\) so that the lines given by the equations \\( y = 2x + 13 \\) and \\( 6y + ax = 6 \\) are perpendicular, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Find the slope of the first line:**\n   The first line is given by the equation \\( y = 2x + 13 \\). This is in slope-intercept form \\( y = mx + b \\), where \\( m \\) is the slope. Therefore, the slope of the first line is \\( 2 \\).\n\n2. **Rewrite the second line in slope-intercept form:**\n   The second line is given by the equation \\( 6y + ax = 6 \\). To find the slope, we need to solve for \\( y \\).\n   \\[\n   6y + ax = 6\n   \\]\n   Subtract \\( ax \\) from both sides:\n   \\[\n   6y = -ax + 6\n   \\]\n   Divide every term by 6:\n   \\[\n   y = -\\frac{a}{6}x + 1\n   \\]\n   Now the second line is in slope-intercept form \\( y = mx + b \\), where the slope \\( m \\) is \\( -\\frac{a}{6} \\).\n\n3. **Determine the condition for perpendicularity:**\n   Two lines are perpendicular if the product of their slopes is \\( -1 \\). Therefore, we set the product of the slopes of the two lines equal to \\( -1 \\):\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot \\left( -\\frac{a}{6} \\right) = -1\n   \\]\n   Simplify the left side:\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{2a}{6} = -1\n   \\]\n   Simplify \\(\\frac{2a}{6}\\) to \\(\\frac{a}{3}\\):\n   \\[\n   -\\frac{a}{3} = -1\n   \\]\n   Multiply both sides by \\(-3\\):\n   \\[\n   a = 3\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the value of \\( a \\) that makes the lines perpendicular is \\(\\boxed{3}\\).",
